        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tocharians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The oases of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> Depression (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>): Kucha (Kuśi), the largest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Agni</a> (Yanqi, Karashar) and Turpan with Qocho (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves">Kizil Caves</a> 65 km west of Kucha; perhaps also Krorän on the southeast edge of the basin.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Farming communities in the oases from c. 2000 BCE, before the Tocharian languages are attested; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> by the 2nd century BCE, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the Han, the Rouran, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> (c. 480–550), the Turks (from c. 560) and the Tang (from the 630s–640s); the languages believed extinct during the 9th century.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Tocharian A and B languages, written in a variant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a>; Buddhist monasteries of the Sarvāstivāda school; the murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves">Kizil Caves</a>; the monk and translator Kumārajīva of Kucha; oasis cities on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>; travel passes on slips of poplar wood; one love poem in Tocharian B</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Some 7,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> recovered from about 30 sites in the northeast Tarim from the early 20th century, named “Tocharian” by Friedrich W. K. Müller; Chinese histories (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>, the Book of Jin) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>’s Records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>; the Kizil murals; studies of the Tarim mummies and ancient DNA.</span></div>
        </div>
