    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Tocharians</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarim-basin.jpg" alt="Tarim Basin" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tarim Basin <span class="kid-tale-cr">NASA, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 2,000 years ago, the Tocharians lived in oasis cities around a great desert in today’s China.</p><p>An oasis is a watered place in a desert; theirs got water from melting mountain snow.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Millet <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kurt Stüber [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They grew millet, wheat, rice and grapes, and raised horses, cattle, sheep and camels.</p><p>A Chinese book says their biggest city, Kucha, had triple walls and a thousand temples.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camel.jpg" alt="Camel" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Camel <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jjron, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their cities stood on the Silk Road, where caravans of traders passed through.</p><p>Caravans carried wooden passes telling the next station how big the caravan was.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Xuanzang <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Most Tocharians became Buddhists, and Kucha was a centre of Buddhist learning.</p><p>The Chinese monk Xuanzang found about a hundred monasteries there, with over 5,000 monks.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kizil-caves.jpg" alt="Kizil Caves" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kizil Caves <span class="kid-tale-cr">Hiroki Ogawa, CC BY 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>At the Kizil Caves, painters filled over 200 cave temples with stories of the Buddha.</p><p>They wrote their two languages in an Indian script, and one love poem survives.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaochang.jpg" alt="Gaochang" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Gaochang <span class="kid-tale-cr">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 1,300 years ago, Chinese armies conquered their cities and killed many people in Kucha.</p><p>Their people mixed with the Uyghurs, who ruled from Gaochang, and spoke Uyghur instead.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Tocharians are known by a borrowed name: scholars called their languages after the Tókharoi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, an identification now generally considered erroneous, and what they called themselves is unknown.</li><li>Their two languages, Tocharian A and Tocharian B, were Indo-European, and by glotto-chronological data closest to Western branches such as proto-Germanic and proto-Italic.</li><li>Their oasis cities, Kucha the largest, were Buddhist centres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>; they never recovered from the Tang conquest of the 640s, and their people blended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, whose language replaced theirs.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Among the everyday texts written in Tocharian are travel passes: small slips of poplar wood telling the officials at the next station along the road the size of the caravan allowed to pass.</p></aside>
    </section>

